For K2, a small sweep of the Sun--planet line satisfies $delta A = (1)/(2) r^2 delta theta$, so
$
  dot(A) = (1)/(2) r^2 dot(theta) = (l)/(2),
$
which is constant by the conservation of angular momentum; this holds for any central force.

For K3, dimensional analysis applies because the only parameter in Newton's equation is $k = G M$: matching $[T] = L^(A + 3B) T^(-2B)$ gives $A = 3\/2$ and $B = -1\/2$, so $T = c R^((3)/(2)) \/ k^(1\/2)$. There is no unique radius associated to an ellipse, but taking any will do.

More precisely, integrating $dot(A) = (l)/(2)$ over the full period,
$
  T = integral_0^T dif t = integral_0^A (2)/(l) dif A = (2)/(l) ppi a b = (2 ppi)/(sqrt(G M)) ((r_0)/(1 - e^2))^((3)/(2)),
$
using $l^2 = k r_0$, where $a b = (r_0^2)/((1-e^2)^((3)/(2)))$. This is $(2 ppi)/(sqrt(G M)) R_"avg"^((3 slash 2))$ for the average radius $R_"avg" = (r_0)/(1-e^2)$ of the ellipse.
